.TH CCOL_CIRCQ_TIMED_RECV_ZC 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_circq_timed_recv_zc \- receive a message with a timeout
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_circq_timed_recv_zc(ccol_circular_queue *cq,
                                   c_message_t *target_buf,
                                   uint64_t timeout_us);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function blocks and waits for a message. It waits for the given
timeout at most. If a message arrives, the function receives it and
transfers the ownership.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cq          Circular queue to receive from
target_buf  Buffer that gets the message
timeout_us  Longest time to wait, in microseconds, from the call. 0 does
            not wait: the call is then ccol_circq_try_recv_zc.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success              - The function received a message
ccol_invalid_args         - cq or target_buf is NULL
ccol_container_empty      - The timeout is 0 and the queue is empty
ccol_timed_out            - The timeout ended
ccol_unexpected_failure   - The wait failed with an error other than a
                            timeout; errno holds that error
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function blocks for the duration of the timeout at most.
On success, the caller gets the ownership and must free
  target_buf->data.
A timeout of 0 does not wait. The call then does exactly what
ccol_circq_try_recv_zc does, and returns its result:
ccol_container_empty, and not ccol_timed_out, when it cannot proceed at
once.
A timeout too large to form a deadline, such as UINT64_MAX, saturates
to the latest deadline that the clock can hold and in practice waits
with no end; it never wraps into the past.
The library measures the timeout against CLOCK_MONOTONIC. A change to the
wall clock of the system does not make the timeout longer or shorter. An
administrator, NTP, or the resume of a virtual machine can change that
clock. The call waits for the duration that the caller gave.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circq_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_try_recv_zc (3)
